  "market_context": "Strong uptrend on GER40 1-hour chart with price making higher highs and higher lows. Multiple regular bearish divergences appeared during the rally, but price only pulled back to the trend line before resuming upward movement.",
  "key_observation": "Several regular bearish divergences occurred where price made higher highs while RSI made lower highs, yet all remained within the 70–30 RSI range and failed to reverse the trend—only producing minor pullbacks to the trend line.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst identified multiple regular bearish divergences on the GER40 1H chart where price made higher highs but RSI made lower highs.",
    "Step 2: He noted that despite these divergences, price only retraced slightly to the rising trend line and then continued upward, confirming the divergence was not respected.",
    "Step 3: He emphasized that these divergences occurred entirely within the 70/30 RSI range (RSI never exceeded 70 or fell below 30), making them low-probability setups.",
    "Step 4: He applied his 'secret sauce' filter: only considering regular divergences where at least one RSI peak is outside the 70/30 range (e.g., above 70 for bearish), and discarded the in-range ones as noise."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Regular bearish divergences within the 70/30 RSI range are unreliable in strong trends; they often produce only shallow pullbacks. Filtering them out prevents counter-trend trades with poor risk-reward.",
